<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mutahharten</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1403">1403</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Taharten</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mutahharten was the ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erzincan">Erzincan</a> in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> in the late 14th and early 15th centuries, after serving as a governor under the Eretnids. He ruled a mixed population of Turks, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> and survived between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> confederations and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> by becoming Timur's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>. The Castilian envoy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruy-gonzalez-de-clavijo">Ruy González de Clavijo</a> described his court in 1404.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mutahharten.jpg" alt="Mutahharten" width="292" height="407" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: by Tulsi with face painted by Mahdu · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Taharten,_the_Prince_of_Arzinjan.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erzincan">Erzincan</a> lay south of the Empire of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trabzon">Trebizond</a> and traded closely with it; most of its people were Christian Armenians under a Muslim ruler. Its earlier <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a>, Ahi Ayna and then Pir Husayn, held it as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> of the Eretnids. Early Ottoman sources call Mutahharten a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatar</a>, while the historian Astarabadi made him a nephew of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eretna">Eretna</a>, and the historian Göde identifies his father as Eretna's brother Burak Beg, appointed emir of Erzincan with Eretna's approval.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Taking power on Pir Husayn's death in 1379, Mutahharten struck his own coins and had the khutbah read in his name. The Eretnid sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ala-al-din-ali">Ala al-Din Ali</a> marched against him; defeated in a first battle, Mutahharten called in the Dulkadirids and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aq-qoyunlu">Aq Qoyunlu</a>, and Ali retreated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sivas">Sivas</a>. Ali's vizier <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kadi-burhan-al-din">Kadi Burhan al-Din</a>, who declared himself sultan in 1381, then fought him for years, razing parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erzincan">Erzincan</a> in 1384–5 before the two agreed to recognise each other's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>.</p><p>Often outmatched, Mutahharten changed sides freely. He executed his brother-in-law and ally Zannun and massacred the people of Koyulhisar, broke with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aq-qoyunlu">Aq Qoyunlu</a> in 1389 and was twice defeated by them. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> arrived in late 1386 he quickly accepted his rule, and in 1393–4 he put Timur's name on his coins and in the khutbah. He defeated Burhan al-Din at Pulur on 27 October 1395, and the war ended only with Burhan al-Din's death in 1398.</p><p>With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trabzon">Trebizond</a> relations stayed close: he took kharāj from the empire and married a daughter of its emperor. Around 1400, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> ordered the Christians of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erzincan">Erzincan</a> massacred after a priest refused to convert, Mutahharten had the order reversed by paying 9,000 aspers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Refusing the Ottoman sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayezid-i">Bayezid I</a>'s demand for submission, he relied on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a>. Bayezid took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erzincan">Erzincan</a> in 1401 and briefly held him and his Trapezuntine wife captive, but he was restored and joined Timur's campaign that ended at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ankara">Battle of Ankara</a>. He died in late 1403 with no surviving legitimate son. His grandson Yar Ali ruled for a time, before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> ceased to exist amid the struggle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aq-qoyunlu">Aq Qoyunlu</a> and Qara Qoyunlu for Erzincan.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mutahharten” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Mutahharten</b> was a governor of Erzincan under the Eretnids.</blockquote><a href="/state/emirate-of-erzincan">Emirate of Erzincan</a></li><li><blockquote>He spent his reign fighting: against the Ottomans, whom he defeated at Kirkdilim in 1392, against the Mamluks, the Aq Qoyunlu, <b>Mutahharten of Erzincan</b> and the beys of Amasya.</blockquote><a href="/state/kadi-burhan-al-din-state">Kadi Burhan al-Din state</a></li></ul></section>
